    "children": [
      {
        "id": "p32-challenge-q8-a",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "a",
        "prompt": "Show that $(a^2+b^2)(c^2+d^2)=(ac+bd)^2+(ad-bc)^2$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "Both sides expand to $a^2c^2+a^2d^2+b^2c^2+b^2d^2$.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(ac+bd)^2+(ad-bc)^2&=a^2c^2+2abcd+b^2d^2+a^2d^2-2abcd+b^2c^2\\\\(ac+bd)^2+(ad-bc)^2&=a^2c^2+a^2d^2+b^2c^2+b^2d^2\\\\(ac+bd)^2+(ad-bc)^2&=a^2(c^2+d^2)+b^2(c^2+d^2)\\\\(ac+bd)^2+(ad-bc)^2&=(a^2+b^2)(c^2+d^2).\\end{align*}$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 42
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-challenge-q8-b",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "b",
        "prompt": "Show that $(x^2+1)(x^2+4)(x^2-2x+2)(x^2+2x+2)=((x^2+2)^2+x^2)(x^4+4)$.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x^2+1)(x^2+4)=(x^2+2)^2+x^2$ and $(x^2-2x+2)(x^2+2x+2)=x^4+4$, giving the stated identity.",
          "worked": "$$\\begin{align*}(x^2+1)(x^2+4)&=x^4+5x^2+4\\\\(x^2+1)(x^2+4)&=x^4+4x^2+4+x^2\\\\(x^2+1)(x^2+4)&=(x^2+2)^2+x^2.\\end{align*}$$\nAlso, using the difference of two squares,\n$$\\begin{align*}(x^2-2x+2)(x^2+2x+2)&=((x^2+2)-2x)((x^2+2)+2x)\\\\(x^2-2x+2)(x^2+2x+2)&=(x^2+2)^2-(2x)^2\\\\(x^2-2x+2)(x^2+2x+2)&=x^4+4x^2+4-4x^2\\\\(x^2-2x+2)(x^2+2x+2)&=x^4+4.\\end{align*}$$\nMultiplying these two identities gives the required result.",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 63
      },
      {
        "id": "p32-challenge-q8-c",
        "type": "part",
        "label": "c",
        "prompt": "Hence, express $(x^2+1)(x^2+4)(x^2-2x+2)(x^2+2x+2)$ as the sum of the squares of two polynomials having integer coefficients.",
        "layout": "list",
        "columns": null,
        "questionDiagrams": [],
        "children": [],
        "answer": {
          "short": "$(x^4+2x^2+2x)^2+(x^3-2x^2-4)^2$.\n\nApply part (a) with $a=x^2+2$, $b=x$, $c=x^2$ and $d=2$.",
          "worked": "By part (b), the required product is $((x^2+2)^2+x^2)((x^2)^2+2^2)$. Apply part (a) with $a=x^2+2$, $b=x$, $c=x^2$ and $d=2$.\n$$\\begin{align*}ac+bd&=(x^2+2)x^2+2x\\\\ac+bd&=x^4+2x^2+2x,\\\\ad-bc&=2(x^2+2)-x\\cdot x^2\\\\ad-bc&=-x^3+2x^2+4.\\end{align*}$$\nTherefore the product is\n$$(x^4+2x^2+2x)^2+(-x^3+2x^2+4)^2.$$\nSince changing the sign of an expression does not change its square, this is\n$$(x^4+2x^2+2x)^2+(x^3-2x^2-4)^2.$$",
          "solutionDiagrams": []
        },
        "answerSpaceMm": 55
      }
    ]
  },
